   "id": "f652766a",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": 
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "472b312c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Encoding to JSON Objects using `Pydantic`\n",
    "\n",
    "The schema that we want to use to validate JSON objects is `{doc_id, source, title, sections[], lang, pub_date, url, doc_type, metadata}`. The first question that came to mind was: **\"Why not just use a Python `dict`?\"** It turns out that while a `dict` is perfectly adequate for storing arbitrary data, it provides no guarantees about the structure or types of that data. Missing fields, misspelled keys, or incorrect types are only discovered when the program attempts to use them, often resulting in runtime errors that are difficult to trace.\n",
    "\n",
    "`Pydantic` helps with this problem by making a plan as a Python class. When you put data into this model it checks the data to make sure it is correct. The model needs certain fields to be there the values need to be the type and it tells you clearly if something is wrong. If the data is not correct Pydantic tells you what is wrong. Pydantic also makes it easy to change Python objects into JSON. This is really useful because you can use Pydantic to make sure your data is good and then easily use reuse it anywhere.\n",
    "We can also use `msgspec` for a light weight and faster experience but We are not doing it for critical systems. Maybe in the future. \n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "510183ab",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "class Section (BaseModel) : \n",
    "    title: str\n",
    "    text: str \n",
    "    grade: str | None = None "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "2a52a690",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "class Document(BaseModel):\n",
    "    doc_id: int\n",
    "    source: str | None \n",
    "    title: str | None\n",
    "    sections: list[Section] = []\n",
    "    lang: str | None = None \n",
    "    pub_date: date | None\n",
    "    url : HttpUrl\n",
    "    doc_type: str  \n",
    "    metadata: dict[str, Any] = {}"
   ]
  }
